 <details>
 <summary>See what each layer does, lab results, and limits</summary>
 
 **Layered like a doctor's visit: take the temperature first, draw blood only if there is a fever, order a scan only if the blood test is off.** Not every tool is switched on at once: some tools change how the app runs, and memory snapshots may capture user data; recording everything when nothing is wrong means paying the highest price every time.
 
 | Layer | Question | How | When to go deeper |
 |---|---|---|---|
 | L0 | Did it get slower? Does it meet the agreed standard? | Run Google's performance test tool, Macrobenchmark, then `lumos macrobench` compares it with the saved baseline and the human-set standards | Slower than the baseline, or below standard |
 | L1 | Where is it stuck? Busy computing, or waiting on something? | Each test run leaves a millisecond-by-millisecond recording (a Perfetto trace), read with the steps of Google's official skill; it also works out whether the time went to the newer (Compose) or older (XML) way of building screens | The app itself is busy → deeper; it is waiting on another program or a file → stay here and follow who made it wait |
 | L2 | Which function, which line? | simpleperf (Android's performance sampling tool) notes "which code is running right now" every millisecond; the line that shows up most is the culprit; phone settings are restored afterwards | That line creates lots of objects → deeper |
-| L3 | What is using memory, and why is it never freed? | Take memory snapshots and find which variable keeps holding on to it | — |
+| L3 | What is using memory, and why is it never freed? | Perfetto's `heap_profile` shows what allocates heavily, and `java_heap_dump` takes memory snapshots to find which variable keeps holding on to it | — |
+
+Tools per layer: L0 is Google's performance test library Macrobenchmark plus `lumos macrobench` for the verdict; L1 is a recording from Google's system tracing tool Perfetto, read with its query tool `trace_processor` following the official skill; L2 is simpleperf from the Android NDK; L3 is Perfetto's `heap_profile` and `java_heap_dump`.
 
 **Why Google's official skill.** Google maintains the steps for reading performance recordings and updates them with its tools, which is more reliable than writing our own. The skill and every tool are each pinned to a fixed version and re-verified on upgrade, so a result found today still matches when the same record is rerun later.
 
-**Memory: catching "the library offers a release method, the app forgets to call it".** Libraries such as players or maps need release to be called when a screen closes; forgetting keeps the memory in use. The project's own UI test opens and closes the same screen on the phone again and again, Lumos's collection script gathers data alongside, and `lumos macrobench` then judges seven things (each one is judged only if the project's standards file has a line for it):
+**Memory: catching "the library offers a release method, the app forgets to call it".** Libraries such as players or maps need release to be called when a screen closes; forgetting keeps the memory in use. The project's own UI test (Android's UI Automator today, Maestro later) opens and closes the same screen on the phone again and again, Lumos's collection script gathers data alongside, and `lumos macrobench` then judges seven things (each one is judged only if the project's standards file has a line for it):
 
-- On the C/C++ side, memory nothing uses any more that was never freed.
-- Objects that keep multiplying each time the screen opens.
-- How many of a named object remain after the screen closes.
-- The highest memory use.
-- Whether the app crashed or was killed by the system.
-- Whether Android's built-in StrictMode check caught an unclosed resource, an unregistered listener, or a screen kept alive. This needs both: the project turns StrictMode on in its test build and declares so in its settings; without the declaration it is skipped. Declared but not actually turned on, it receives no violations and is wrongly judged a pass.
-- Whether the screen froze for more than 0.7 seconds.
+- On the C/C++ side, memory nothing uses any more that was never freed (Android's built-in `dumpsys meminfo --unreachable`, scanned before and after).
+- Objects that keep multiplying each time the screen opens (Perfetto's `java_heap_dump` takes a snapshot before and after, and `trace_processor` counts each kind of object; snapshots are taken only after test data only is declared).
+- How many of a named object remain after the screen closes (from the second snapshot above).
+- The highest memory use (Perfetto records the whole open-close run, and `trace_processor` finds the maximum).
+- Whether the app crashed or was killed by the system (Android's built-in `dumpsys activity exit-info` before and after, plus a check that the process did not change).
+- Whether Android's built-in StrictMode check caught an unclosed resource, an unregistered listener, or a screen kept alive (`adb logcat` collects this app's log during the run and keeps only the violation lines). This needs both: the project turns StrictMode on in its test build and declares so in its settings; without the declaration it is skipped. Declared but not actually turned on, it receives no violations and is wrongly judged a pass.
+- Whether the screen froze for more than 0.7 seconds (frame times from the same Perfetto recording).
 
 When one of the first four (the memory ones) fails, it jumps straight to L3: the two middle layers look at where CPU time goes, and memory problems do not show there; the project must first declare test data only, otherwise it stops at L0 and records the gap. A frozen screen goes through L1 as usual; crashes and StrictMode violations are read straight from the collected exit records and log.
 
 **Every step goes into the evidence record.** Phone model and state, the installed app (matched by file fingerprint), each query with its expected result, and why the next layer was needed all sit in one folder, with a `rerun.sh` that reruns every query with an expected result on the same raw data and compares the results in one go; the raw data stays on the local machine and is not committed. Before committing, a separate AI that knows nothing of the background re-judges every conclusion from this record alone; if it cannot, the record is not clear enough.
 
 **Lab results** (one Samsung tablet (model SM-X710) and a small app with deliberately planted problems):
 
 - Each list row deliberately waits 8 ms longer: L0 reports the screen got slower (each redraw of the screen is a frame; for the slower tenth of frames, processor time per frame went from 4.3 ms to 10.4 ms), and L2 points straight to line 36 of the lab app's `MainActivity.kt`.
 - A third-party library written in Java, not released when the screen closes: 5 objects left behind; L3 shows the library's own internal list holds them.
 - A third-party library written in C/C++, not released: 10 MB extra (5 open-close rounds, 2 MB each), traced to the library file and the app function that created it.
diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index bf270072..0fc8db0b 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -314,33 +314,35 @@ Android App 改完之後，Lumos 在真的手機或平板上確認兩件事：**
 <details>
 <summary>看每一層在做什麼、實驗結果與限制</summary>
 
 **像看病一樣分層：先量體溫，有發燒才抽血，抽血有問題才照片子。** 不一開始就把所有工具全開，因為有些工具本身會讓 App 跑起來不一樣，記憶體快照還可能拍到使用者資料；沒問題卻全部都錄，等於每次都付最高的代價。
 
 | 層 | 要回答的問題 | 怎麼查 | 什麼情況才往下 |
 |---|---|---|---|
 | L0 | 有沒有變慢？有沒有達到約定的標準？ | 跑 Google 的效能測試工具 Macrobenchmark，再用 `lumos macrobench` 跟上次存的基準比，並對照人訂的標準 | 比基準慢、或沒達標 |
 | L1 | 卡在哪？是忙著計算，還是在等別人？ | 每次測試都會留下一份逐毫秒的執行紀錄（Perfetto Trace），照 Google 官方 skill 的步驟讀它；同時算出時間花在新式（Compose）還是舊式（XML）的畫面寫法上 | App 自己在忙著算 → 往下；是在等別的程式或檔案 → 留在這層，順著追是誰讓它等 |
 | L2 | 是哪個函式、哪一行？ | 用 simpleperf（Android 附的效能抽樣工具）每毫秒記一次「現在在跑哪段程式」，次數最多的那一行就是兇手；查完把手機設定還原 | 那一行在大量建立物件 → 往下 |
-| L3 | 誰在佔記憶體？為什麼一直不釋放？ | 拍記憶體快照，找出是哪個變數一直抓著那些東西 | — |
+| L3 | 誰在佔記憶體？為什麼一直不釋放？ | 用 Perfetto 附的 `heap_profile` 看誰在大量配置記憶體、`java_heap_dump` 拍記憶體快照，找出是哪個變數一直抓著那些東西 | — |
+
+各層用的工具：L0 是 Google 的效能測試函式庫 Macrobenchmark，加上 `lumos macrobench` 判定；L1 是 Google 的系統追蹤工具 Perfetto 錄下的紀錄，用它的查詢工具 `trace_processor` 照官方 skill 讀；L2 是 Android NDK 附的 simpleperf；L3 是 Perfetto 附的 `heap_profile` 與 `java_heap_dump`。
 
 **為什麼用 Google 官方的 skill。** 讀效能紀錄的步驟由 Google 維護，會跟著它的工具一起更新，比我們自己寫一套可靠。我們把 skill 和每個工具各自固定在一個版本，升級時要重新驗過；這樣今天查到的結果，日後照同一份紀錄重跑還是對得上。
 
-**記憶體：專抓「第三方套件有提供釋放的方法，App 卻忘了呼叫」。** 例如播放器、地圖這類套件，關掉畫面時要呼叫 release，忘了就會一直佔著記憶體。專案自己寫的操作測試會在手機上反覆打開又關掉同一個畫面，Lumos 的收集腳本在旁邊收資料，再由 `lumos macrobench` 判斷七件事（每一項都要在專案的標準檔裡寫一條才會判）：
+**記憶體：專抓「第三方套件有提供釋放的方法，App 卻忘了呼叫」。** 例如播放器、地圖這類套件，關掉畫面時要呼叫 release，忘了就會一直佔著記憶體。專案自己寫的操作測試（現在用 Android 的 UI Automator，之後換成 Maestro）會在手機上反覆打開又關掉同一個畫面，Lumos 的收集腳本在旁邊收資料，再由 `lumos macrobench` 判斷七件事（每一項都要在專案的標準檔裡寫一條才會判）：
 
-- C/C++ 那一側有沒有記憶體已經沒人在用、卻沒被釋放。
-- 某些物件的數量是不是越開越多。
-- 指定的物件關掉畫面後還剩幾個。
-- 記憶體用量最高到多少。
-- App 有沒有閃退或被系統強制關掉。
-- 系統內建的檢查（StrictMode）有沒有抓到資源沒關、註冊沒取消、畫面被留住這類違規。這項要兩件事都做：專案在測試版自己打開 StrictMode，並在設定檔宣告有打開；沒宣告就列成跳過。宣告了卻沒真的打開，會因為一筆違規都收不到而被誤判成通過。
-- 畫面有沒有卡住超過 0.7 秒。
+- C/C++ 那一側有沒有記憶體已經沒人在用、卻沒被釋放（用 Android 內建的 `dumpsys meminfo --unreachable`，開關前後各掃一次）。
+- 某些物件的數量是不是越開越多（用 Perfetto 的 `java_heap_dump` 在開關前後各拍一份快照，再用 `trace_processor` 數每種物件；要先宣告只用測試資料才會拍）。
+- 指定的物件關掉畫面後還剩幾個（同上一項的第二份快照）。
+- 記憶體用量最高到多少（開關期間用 Perfetto 全程錄製，再用 `trace_processor` 算最大值）。
+- App 有沒有閃退或被系統強制關掉（用 Android 內建的 `dumpsys activity exit-info` 讀前後兩次的結束紀錄，並比對行程有沒有換過）。
+- 系統內建的檢查（StrictMode）有沒有抓到資源沒關、註冊沒取消、畫面被留住這類違規（開關期間用 `adb logcat` 收這個 App 的日誌，只留違規那幾行）。這項要兩件事都做：專案在測試版自己打開 StrictMode，並在設定檔宣告有打開；沒宣告就列成跳過。宣告了卻沒真的打開，會因為一筆違規都收不到而被誤判成通過。
+- 畫面有沒有卡住超過 0.7 秒（同一份 Perfetto 錄製裡的每幀時間）。
 
 前四項（記憶體類）沒過時，會直接跳到 L3：中間兩層看的是 CPU 花在哪裡，記憶體問題在那裡看不出來；不過專案要先宣告只用測試資料，沒宣告就停在 L0 並記下缺口。畫面卡住照一般流程從 L1 查；閃退與 StrictMode 違規則直接看收集到的結束紀錄與日誌。
 
 **每一步都寫進查證紀錄。** 手機型號與狀態、安裝的 App（用檔案指紋比對）、每一條查詢與預期結果、為什麼要往下一層，全都放在同一個資料夾，附一支 `rerun.sh`，可以對同一批原始資料一鍵重跑每條標了預期結果的查詢、比對結果；原始資料只留在本機，不進版控。提交前會另外找一個完全不知道前因後果的 AI，只看這份紀錄重新判斷每個結論；它判斷不出來，就代表紀錄寫得不夠清楚。
 
 **實驗結果**（用一台 Samsung 平板（型號 SM-X710），和一個刻意埋了問題的小 App）：
 
 - 清單每畫一列就故意多等 8 毫秒：L0 報出畫面變慢（畫面每重畫一次叫一幀；較慢的那一成幀，每幀花的處理器時間從 4.3 毫秒變成 10.4 毫秒），L2 直接指到實驗 App 的 `MainActivity.kt` 第 36 行。
 - Java 寫的第三方套件，關畫面時沒呼叫 release：多留下 5 個物件，L3 查出是被套件自己內部的清單抓著。
 - C/C++ 寫的第三方套件沒呼叫 release：多佔 10 MB（開關 5 次、每次 2 MB），指到是哪個套件檔，以及 App 裡哪個函式建立它。
